# Bridge — probe, report, hand back
## Build the shared cross-runtime package
For the current jstack source checkout, use `node dist/packaging/build.js build
--source SOURCE_ROOT --output NEW_DIRECTORY`. This single package contains two
native manifests, reviewed host entries, and a complete shared `payload/` tree.
`node dist/packaging/build.js check PACKAGE` verifies its declared file membership,
content receipt, public names, and private-data boundary. Build output must be
new; source and user-home settings are not modified. Both host entries set
JSTACK_PLUGIN_ROOT to the installed payload. Check actual host discovery and run
from the installed cache with source access denied before reporting portability.
The `scripts/export.js` path below is retained only for legacy migration and
regression comparison. Do not use it as the new default or apply its outer-root
assumptions to the dual-host package. Rebuild from the source checkout, never
edit generated entries or silently rewrite one host's tools into another's.
## Three states, not two
"Installed or not" is the wrong shape. Most tools are partly there: the binary
exists but a runtime is missing, or the CLI is present but not authenticated.
Collapsing that into a boolean turns a fixable gap into an unexplained failure
halfway through the work.
Every probe script in this plugin exits with one of three codes:
| Exit | Meaning | What the caller does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 0 | Fully available | Proceed |
| 1 | Partially available | Proceed on the reduced path, and say which capability is gone |
| 2 | Blocked | Stop, and print install guidance |
Code 1 is the one that earns the convention. Without it every partial
environment either pretends to work or refuses to start, and both hide the same
information: exactly which capability is missing.
## Never install on the user's behalf
Print the command; do not run it. Installing changes a machine the user
maintains, in a way this session did not scope and cannot undo cleanly. Package
managers pull dependencies, write outside the project, and sometimes require
elevation — none of that is inside "help me with this task".
The output on exit 2 is the install command, in full, ready to paste. Guidance
that says "install X" without the command is not guidance.
## Probe at the point of use, not at startup
Checking everything up front makes every session pay for tools that session
will not touch, and the check goes stale the moment the environment changes.
Probe when the capability is about to be used, so the result is current and the
cost falls on the path that needs it.
## Degrade by naming what is lost
A reduced path is legitimate. A reduced path that does not say it is reduced is
a silent downgrade, and the user reads the output as if the full tool ran.
Say which capability is gone and what that means for the result — not "running
in limited mode", which names nothing. `references/probe-recipes.md` holds the
probe shapes and the phrasing for each state, and `scripts/probe.js` is the
implementation those recipes describe — run it rather than re-deriving a probe.
## Do not wrap what the tool already does
A bridge is a probe plus a call. When it starts reformatting arguments,
reordering flags, or re-implementing a subcommand, it has become a second
interface to maintain — and it will drift from the first one on the tool's next
release. The `upstream` skill covers where that line sits.
Pass the user's arguments through. If the tool's own error message is the
clearest thing available, show it rather than replacing it with a friendlier one
that says less.
## Installing this plugin's skills into another agent
For native Codex plugin delivery, use the shared package above:
`node dist/packaging/build.js build` produces both hosts, and
`node dist/packaging/release.js --source . --output NEW_DIRECTORY` creates a
checked local marketplace for both. The build selects the reviewed Codex bodies
under `hosts/codex/skills/` when the host semantics differ and keeps the plugin's
runtime dependencies together, without registering a marketplace remotely or
changing the user's installed skills. `node dist/packaging/build.js check PACKAGE`
detects payload drift; this local receipt proves byte consistency, not publisher
authenticity. `scripts/export.js` stays a legacy migration utility only.
Exercise the built package after moving it and without the source checkout.
In each outer entry, relative `scripts/` and `references/` paths resolve under
`<outer-root>/payload/skills/<skill-name>/`; use `JSTACK_PLUGIN_ROOT` for the
plugin-level payload root.
Absolute build paths are not portable. Claude commands, agents and automatic
hook registration are excluded from Codex delivery; native skills and explicit
verification commands supply the supported execution path. The flat-skill installer below remains a migration utility.
`scripts/install.js` links or copies this marketplace's skills into another
agent's skill folder, and `scripts/rules.json` holds the mechanical notation
substitutions it applies on the way. Four of its rules are the ones that get
re-broken, and each of them fails silently.
**The marketplace root is found by marker, not by path arithmetic.** Counting
directories upward bakes the script's own depth into its behaviour, so moving
the script one level makes it scan a different tree. The scan then comes back
empty, every entry in the lockfile looks like a source that vanished, and the
installer deletes the installed skills while reporting success. It walks upward
for `.claude-plugin/marketplace.json` instead, and refuses to reclaim anything
when an empty scan meets a non-empty lockfile.
**The installer excludes itself by skill directory, not by plugin name.**
Otherwise it would install itself into the target as one more skill.
**`${JSTACK_PLUGIN_ROOT}` does not survive a symlink.** The other agent never
sets that variable, so a skill that reaches its own scripts through the token
must be rewritten with the absolute plugin root rather than linked. Linked, the
skill arrives whole and every path inside it is dead.
**Classification covers the runtime tree, not only `SKILL.md`.** A clean root
can still send the reader to a Claude-only reference or run a script whose path
was never rewritten. The installer scans every non-test UTF-8 reference and
script. Mechanical notation changes are applied to each affected copied file;
a semantic blocker anywhere in that runtime tree blocks the skill until the
installed path is safe.
**A destination name has one source and ownership must be proved.** Codex skills
share one flat namespace. If two plugins provide the same skill name, neither
may overwrite the other. Before replacing or reclaiming any existing directory
or symlink, the installer matches the lock record to the link target or the
generated-file header. A user-owned link is preserved even when its basename
matches a marketplace skill.
**Substitution is for notation, never for a quoted literal.** A document that
names a forbidden string is stating a rule about that exact string. Rewriting it
forbids something that does not exist and leaves the real string unforbidden,
and the checker that ships beside the document keeps enforcing the original — so
the two disagree with nobody being told.
## Task routing and completion
For authorized delegation, use the common routing contract in
[Runtime routing](../../runtime/ROUTING.md). Run `jst route plan --request FILE`,
then `jst route run --decision FILE` to reserve one attempt. A native decision
is executed through the current session tools; it is not a CLI substitution.
Record independently checked artifacts with `jst route record`. If `jstack` is not
found, follow [jst command on PATH](../../runtime/CLI-PATH.md). Retain explicit
user model choices, permission boundaries, exclusive files, and host fork limits.
Only a verification failure permits one model transition; environment failures
do not justify stronger models. Small tasks stay with the coordinator.
## Boundary
Writing the skill that uses the bridge is the `author` skill; deciding whether
to vendor the tool instead of depending on it is the `upstream` skill. This
skill covers the probe and the handoff.
